course: everyday
lesson: 55
title: Bullying and how to stop it
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Bullying is when someone hurts another person on purpose, again and again, and the person being hurt finds it hard to make it stop; it can be hitting or pushing, name-calling, leaving someone out on purpose, spreading rumours, or mean messages online.
explain: One quarrel between friends, or someone bumping you by accident, is not bullying, though it can still need sorting out; bullying is repeated and on purpose.
explain: If you are bullied, it is never your fault: say firmly «Stop, I don't like that», walk away, stay near friends and adults, and tell a trusted adult, and keep telling until somebody helps.
explain: Telling is not tattling: tattling is trying to get someone into trouble, while telling is trying to get someone out of trouble or keep them safe.
explain: If you see bullying, don't join in or laugh; stand with the child who is hurt, invite them to be with you, and tell an adult; when one boy in a Seattle playground said «Leave him alone, come with us», the teasing stopped, because bullies want an audience.
explain: A girl in Ohio was teased every day for months and told no one; she became so unhappy that she did not want to go to school at all, until a teacher noticed and stopped it.
explain: A boy in Sydney watched others push a smaller boy into lockers and did nothing; when he told a teacher at last, he learnt that it had been going on for weeks.
fact: Bullying is hurting someone on purpose, again and again.
fact: Bullying can be physical, with words, by leaving someone out, or online.
fact: A single argument between friends is not the same as bullying.
fact: Being bullied is never the fault of the child who is bullied, and nobody deserves to be bullied for being different.
fact: Bullied children often feel scared or ashamed to tell, even though it is not their fault.
fact: Bullies pick on children who are alone more often, so staying near friends and adults helps.
fact: Telling a trusted adult about bullying is the right thing to do.
fact: Telling keeps someone safe, while tattling only tries to get someone into trouble.
fact: If the first adult does not help, you tell another adult.
fact: Hitting back can get you hurt and can make the bullying worse.
fact: Bystanders, the people who see bullying happen, help the bully if they join in or laugh.
fact: Boys and girls can be friends, and teasing someone for who they play with is unkind.
fact: Standing with the hurt child and getting an adult can help stop bullying.
fact: Schools have rules against bullying, and teachers must act when they know about it.
fact: Spreading rumours and sending mean messages online are forms of bullying.
fact: Bullying can make children anxious, sad and afraid to go to school for a long time.
fact: Bullying usually goes on until someone tells an adult who can stop it.
exercise: Which of these is bullying? | A boy is called names by the same group every day | Two friends argue once about the rules of a game | A girl bumps into you by accident | Your friend does not want to play with you today, just this once
exercise: What makes bullying different from a single quarrel? | It is done on purpose, again and again | It always involves hitting and kicking somebody | It only happens to younger children | It only happens online
exercise: Whose fault is bullying? | The person doing the bullying | The child who is bullied | The child's parents | Nobody's, it is just a game
exercise: What is the difference between telling and tattling? | Telling keeps someone safe; tattling gets someone in trouble | They are exactly the same thing | Telling is to a teacher, tattling is to a parent or another grown-up | Tattling is true and telling is made up
exercise: A child keeps pushing you in the line. What is a good first thing to say? | Stop it, I don't like that | Sorry for being in your way | Nothing, just let it happen | I will push you twice as hard
exercise: Why is hitting back a bad plan? | You can get hurt and the bullying can get worse | It is the fastest way to end bullying for good | Bullies always stop when hit once | It is allowed if they hit first
exercise: You told a teacher about bullying, but nothing changed. What should you do? | Tell another trusted adult, such as a parent | Give up, because telling adults never works anyway | Start bullying the bully | Keep it secret from then on
exercise: Why does laughing at someone being teased help the bully? | Bullies want an audience, and laughter is one | Laughing makes the bully feel bored | Laughing makes the teasing stop | It does not matter if you only laugh a little
exercise: You see a child being left out on purpose every day. What can you do? | Invite them to join you and tell an adult | Leave them alone so you are not left out too | Join in so the bully likes you | Wait and see if it stops after a year
exercise: Which of these is a kind of bullying? | Spreading a nasty rumour about someone | Telling someone their shoelace is untied | Choosing to sit with a different friend once | Disagreeing with someone in a class debate
exercise: Why should a bullied child stay near friends and adults? | Bullies pick on children who are alone more often | Friends will always fight the bully for them | Adults like children who stay near them | It is a school rule for every break and every lunchtime
exercise: Are mean messages sent again and again online bullying? | Yes, online bullying is bullying too | No, because nobody is touched | Only if they are sent at school | Only if the sender uses their real name
exercise: Why can it be hard for a bullied child to tell? | They may feel scared or ashamed, though it is not their fault | Because telling is against school rules | Because adults never believe what children tell them about anything | Because bullying always stops on its own
exercise: How can bystanders stop bullying? | By refusing to join in, standing with the target and telling an adult | By watching quietly from a distance | By filming it to show their friends | By laughing along so that the bully gets bored, finishes faster and goes away
test: Every day at lunch in Leeds, two older girls take Ella's snack and laugh. What is this? | Bullying, because it is on purpose and keeps happening | A joke, because they laugh | A fair trade, because they are older | Nothing to worry about, as long as it is only a snack and not money
test: Jack tells the teacher that Sam keeps getting shoved in the toilets. Is Jack tattling? | No, he is telling to keep Sam safe | Yes, because he is talking about someone | Yes, because Sam did not ask him to | No, because tattling is only about lessons
test: A boy is called names because of his accent. Which is true? | It is not his fault, and he should tell a trusted adult | He should change his accent to stop it | He should stay quiet and say nothing so it does not get worse | He should call them worse names back
test: What would most likely happen if everybody who saw bullying told an adult? | Bullying would be noticed and stopped much sooner | Bullies would get more attention and feel proud | The adults would be too busy to help anyone | Nothing would change at all
test: Your friend tells you she is being bullied but begs you not to tell anyone. What should you do? | Help her tell a trusted adult, even though she is scared | Keep the secret, because she asked you to and a friend never breaks a promise | Tell the bully to stop by fighting them | Tell her to ignore it until it stops
test: Which is NOT bullying? | Two friends who argue once and make up | Kicking someone every day after school | Telling everyone a lie about a classmate | Leaving the same child out of every game on purpose
test: Why is it brave to stand next to a child who is being teased? | It shows the bully that the child is not alone | It means you will win any fight | It makes you the most popular child in the class | It is not brave, only silly
test: A bullied girl thinks it happens because she is different. What is true? | Nobody deserves to be bullied for being different | Being different gives others a reason to bully | She should try to be exactly like everyone else | Bullying is fine if the difference is big
test: What should a school do when it learns about bullying? | Act to stop it and keep the child safe | Wait for the children to sort it out | Tell the bullied child to toughen up | Move the bullied child to another school
test: kid keeps calling me names every day at lunch, been weeks. is that bullying | Yes, hurting on purpose again and again is bullying | No, it is only bullying if he hits you | No, name calling is just a joke | Only if a teacher has already seen it and written it down
test: told miss about it, she said "just ignore it". now what | Tell another trusted adult until someone helps | Give up, telling does not work | Hit him back so he stops | Stay home from school for good and never go back there again
test: gonna punch him back tmrw, dad said stand up for urself | Hitting back can get you hurt and make it worse | Yes, punching back always ends bullying for good | Yes, it is fine if he started it | Only punch him when no teacher looks
test: everyone laughed when they took Sam's bag. i didnt do anything so its fine right | No, laughing along helps the bully, so stand with Sam | Yes, only the bag taker did wrong | Yes, staying out of it is always best, it's not your bag | No, you should take his bag too
test: they've been picking on me for weeks, if i tell it'll get worse | Tell a trusted adult, they can stop it | Keep quiet and hope it ends by itself soon | Fight back alone | Stay home from school forever
test: watching them push the new kid around, not my problem right | Tell an adult and be kind to the new kid | Walk away and forget it | Join in so they leave you alone and pick on him instead | Film it and post it
test: Jack says bullying is just part of growing up. right? | No, bullying hurts and should be stopped | Yes, everyone goes through it | Yes, it makes you tough | Yes, unless it is every day for months and months
test: everyone's saying the bullying stopped cos Josh hit the bully back, so hitting back works | One story proves nothing; hitting back can make it worse, so tell an adult | It proves hitting back always works | It means teachers never help | It shows bullies only stop when someone finally hits them hard enough to scare them
test: heard Leah got bullied cos she's weird, like it's her own fault | Wrong; being bullied is never the fault of the child who is bullied | It is true, weird kids cause it | It is true, if she does not change | It is true, but only online
test: someone's sharing a mean meme about Tom, everyone's laughing, should i send it on | No; sending it on helps the bully, so do not share it and tell an adult | Yes, because everyone has seen it | Yes, as long as you do not laugh | Yes, but only to one friend, and tell them not to send it to anyone else at all
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises bullying? | The repeated and deliberate hurting of a person | A single argument between friends | Any disagreement over a game | An accidental bump in a corridor
exam: Identify the distinction between telling and tattling. | Telling seeks to keep a person safe, whereas tattling seeks only to get a person into trouble | Telling and tattling are identical | Telling is done only by teachers | Tattling is done only to protect others, whereas telling is done in order to punish them severely
exam: It being given that the first adult informed of bullying renders no assistance, select the course of action consistent with established guidance. | To inform another adult | To remain silent thereafter | To deal with the bully by force | To change school at once
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning bystanders. | Bystanders who join in or laugh assist the bully | Bystanders have no effect of any kind | Bystanders who laugh discourage the bully from continuing | Bystanders cannot be involved in bullying
test: they keep saying "Tom loves Lucy" every day n Lucy cries now | They tease that Tom wants her as a girlfriend; every day is bullying, so tell an adult | It is only true love, so it is fine | Join in so they leave you alone | Tell Lucy to stop crying, it's only a joke and they'll get bored of it by next week
test: kid shouting "your dad left ur mum for someone else, everyone knows" at Sam | Saying it to hurt Sam is bullying, so stand by Sam and tell an adult | It is just news, so it is fine | Sam should shout something worse back | Ask the kid for more details about it, so you know if it's true and can tell everyone
test: Mia keeps calling Ollie "girlfriend" cos he plays with girls lol | She is teasing him to make him feel bad; boys and girls can be friends, so tell her to stop | It means Ollie is Mia's boyfriend | It is a fair name, so use it too | Ollie should stop playing with girls so the name goes away and he gets more boy friends
